    },
    {
      "unresolved": false,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "6c5274ba_1eb11f5e",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 12,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002790
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T11:38:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "This is the platform schema rather than a board one: the records live under schemas/platform/, the configuration under configurations/platform/, and they are exported at /xyz/openbmc_project/inventory/system/platform/\u003cplatform\u003e/ rather than under a board [1].\n\nWhich board is reached through which bridge is not a fact any one board has. The platform is the layer that knows how the boards are wired together, so that is where I would expect it to be described.\n\n[1] https://gerrit.openbmc.org/c/openbmc/entity-manager/+/87415",
      "parentUuid": "720d79f3_1d851d1f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 10,
        "startChar": 66,
        "endLine": 12,
        "endChar": 56
      },
      "revId": "9215f32088ed58f2100c004b43ae10214d35043b",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "9a7b2754_e29ce61f",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 16,
      "author": {
        "id": 1000009
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T08:00:30Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "That\u0027s encoding the bridge\u0027s endpoint assignment policy in the config, where the assignment policy is just an implementation detail of the bridge firmware. That seems too brittle for the purpose of these configs.",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 15,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 16,
        "endChar": 42
      },
      "revId": "9215f32088ed58f2100c004b43ae10214d35043b",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
    {
      "unresolved": true,
      "key": {
        "uuid": "aaec66c1_7ba00f1e",
        "filename": "/COMMIT_MSG",
        "patchSetId": 4
      },
      "lineNbr": 16,
      "author": {
        "id": 1002790
      },
      "writtenOn": "2026-08-24T11:38:31Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "You are right. It is what is available today: nothing in a reply says which device answered, and mctpd reports PoolStart and PoolEnd but not the downstream address an EID was assigned through, so position is the only thing tying a record to a pool EID. A consumer can check the number of records against the pool size, which it does, but not their order.\n\nI have narrowed the claim - the order only matters where a pool holds more than one EID, and a pool of one has nothing to order - and made the assumption visible at runtime rather than leaving it in the schema: a bridge with more than one pool EID now logs that it is pairing by position. That annotates the brittleness rather than removing it.\n\nIf mctpd reported the downstream address each pool EID was assigned through, a record could name that address instead of a position, which would be topology rather than policy. Would that be worth having?",
      "parentUuid": "9a7b2754_e29ce61f",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 15,
        "startChar": 24,
        "endLine": 16,
        "endChar": 42
      },
      "revId": "9215f32088ed58f2100c004b43ae10214d35043b",
      "serverId": "adbd0a64-1f21-4d83-b585-671fe73cb6e4"
    },
